Rules for mathematical reasoning
Mathematical work needs stricter discipline than ordinary chat.
The rules I settled on are:

give the result first, then the derivation;
challenge important assumptions;
distinguish established results from numerical checks and speculation;
search the literature when a result may already be known;
keep alternative ideas after the main argument rather than mixing them into it.

The literature rule is particularly important.
AI models are good at proposing ideas that feel new. In mathematics, that is not enough. A useful workflow should automatically ask:

Is this already known?

That does not eliminate hallucinations, but it makes them easier to catch.

Local models
Local models need the same behavioural context.
The rules can be supplied through:

a persistent system prompt in the client;
a model configuration;
a wrapper around the local model;
an agent configuration file.

For example, an Ollama-style model configuration may contain a persistent system prompt:
FROM &lt;base-model&gt;

SYSTEM """
&lt;paste CORE rules here&gt;
"""
A coding-specific local model can receive:
CORE + CODING
while a mathematical research model can receive:
CORE + MATH-RESEARCH
The rules do not change the model weights. They define the working context.

What this solves
The system does not make AI perfectly reliable.
It does solve several practical problems:

answers become denser and easier to read;
useful side ideas are preserved without hijacking the main discussion;
speculative claims are easier to identify;
file edits become less destructive;
coding agents are less likely to consume expensive models unnecessarily;
long chats are less likely to become the sole repository of important information;
behaviour stays consistent across AI products;
rules evolve in a controlled, auditable way.

Most importantly, it changes the relationship with AI.
Instead of repeatedly telling each model how to behave, I maintain one small specification of how I want to work.
The models can change.
The workflow remains mine.

Tests are necessary, but tests can certify the wrong thing
AI is very good at producing tests.
That is useful, but it creates another trap: the presence of tests can create an impression of correctness without asking whether the tests protect the right property.
There is a big difference between:
the test passes
and:
the test validates the mathematical property we actually care about
A test can reproduce the implementation’s own assumptions.
For example, an “independent” validation routine is not truly independent if it imports the same thresholding convention from the algorithm it is checking.
Likewise, a test for an upper bound is not really testing an upper bound if it allows the computed number to fall slightly below the reference value.
This changed the way I think about AI-generated tests.
The best tests should target invariants:

known closed-form solutions;
scale invariance;
conservation laws;
independently derived identities;
monotonicity where theory guarantees it;
explicit failure behaviour;
agreement between two genuinely different formulations.

Those tests are much harder for an incorrect implementation to “agree with accidentally.”

Writing and professional work
The blank page is increasingly unnecessary.
rough thoughts
     ↓
structure
     ↓
draft
     ↓
critique
     ↓
rewrite
     ↓
finished document
Useful outputs include:

reports;
proposals;
presentations;
CVs;
applications;
documentation;
website copy;
technical explanations;
meeting summaries.

The important improvement is that agentic tools can extend this into actual editable files and workflows, rather than stopping at generated prose in a chat window.
This applies beyond code. The same “no copy-paste” principle from the VS Code discussion above works for ordinary long-form writing too: ChatGPT’s macOS “Work with Apps” reads and edits the open document directly in a text editor or notes app, generating a reviewable diff rather than requiring a round trip through the chat window. For long documents specifically — papers, reports, LaTeX source — the useful property is that the model can see the whole open file as context, not just the fragment I remembered to paste in.
For LaTeX specifically, VS Code’s LaTeX Workshop extension (with a TeX distribution installed locally) gives live PDF preview, compile-on-save and click-to-source-line navigation — combined with an in-editor agent, this covers the full loop of drafting, editing and compiling a paper without leaving the editor.

What I delegate
There is a large amount of technical work between having an idea and deciding what it means.
That middle is where I find AI most valuable.
Typical uses include:

literature reconnaissance and source discovery;
comparing papers, assumptions and methods;
summarising unfamiliar technical material before reading it closely;
exploring alternative formulations or special cases;
generating small numerical experiments;
explaining an unfamiliar codebase;
tracing bugs and edge cases;
implementing tightly scoped changes;
proposing regression tests;
reviewing code and documentation for inconsistencies;
checking whether several representations of a project still agree;
restructuring technical writing for different audiences;
converting material between Markdown, Quarto, LaTeX and code;
extracting decisions, unresolved questions and reusable notes from long working sessions.

Individually, many of these tasks are mundane. Collectively, they consume a great deal of time and attention.
Making them cheaper leaves more time for the parts of the work where judgment matters most.

Tests should protect ideas, not just code
AI is very good at generating tests.
That is valuable, but a large test suite can create false confidence if the tests merely repeat the assumptions of the implementation.
For numerical and mathematical software, I prefer tests based on independent properties:

a known closed-form solution;
scale invariance;
a conserved quantity;
a theoretical bound;
monotonicity where theory genuinely guarantees it;
agreement with an independently implemented calculation;
explicit failure when convergence is not achieved.

The question is not simply:

Does the test pass?

It is:

Would this test still expose the error if the implementation and its internal conventions were both wrong in the same way?

That is a much stronger standard.

Where this leaves me
I expect AI systems to become much more capable.
That makes me more interested in using them, not less.
But as production becomes cheaper, other parts of technical work become relatively more important:

choosing worthwhile questions;
making assumptions explicit;
selecting authoritative evidence;
designing meaningful experiments;
noticing when complexity adds nothing;
validating results;
communicating uncertainty;
deciding when the work is finished.

The opportunity is not simply to automate the work I already do.
It is to spend less time on the mechanical middle and more time on those decisions.
Delegate production. Preserve evidence. Verify what matters. Retain ownership of the decision.

Figure provenance
Every displayed figure is generated in base R from repository data and model objects:

01_lambda_estimates.png — Table 1 odds-implied exponential rates;
02_team_effects.png — Table 3 constructor coefficients and 95% intervals;
03_driver_vs_team.png — descriptive 2022 driver averages by constructor;
04_teammate_gaps.png — Section 5 odds-implied within-team gaps.

The exact plotting function for each figure lives in R/figures/ and is included verbatim in the Quarto pages inside collapsed code blocks. The PNGs are committed so the documentation can render without rerunning R, but Rscript analysis/generate_figures.R is the canonical regeneration command.
Figure 3 is intentionally labelled as descriptive. It visualises the raw 2022 averages but is not the final driver-versus-car comparison. Figure 4 is the correct Section 5 calculation, using 2023 Qatar odds-implied expected ranks.

Validation and limitations
tests/run_tests.R checks the published probability column, Table 1 scaled rates, Table 2 values, Table 3 coefficients and \(R^2\), the confidence interval, the descriptive driver averages, and the final pair of flagged drivers.
The reproduction validates the arithmetic and code path; it does not remove the modelling assumptions. In particular:

finishing times are assumed independent in the exponential model;
ranks use a Gaussian approximation and a common variance;
bookmaker odds contain market information and are not a pure skill measure;
stepwise model selection can be unstable and should be interpreted as part of the paper’s specified procedure, not as a universally preferred method;
retirements and incomplete race distances are not explicitly modelled.
